## The SELinux half of the arrangement in docs/system.md.
##
## The control plane hands the few commands its sandbox forbids to systemd,
## and `systemd-run --pipe` passes the daemon's standard input, output, and
## error to the bus *as file descriptors* — that is how `zpool`'s own words
## come back, and how a password reaches `chpasswd` without ever being an
## argument. Those descriptors are pipes, and they carry the daemon's own
## label.
##
## `dbus-broker` has to accept them to relay the call. Stock policy does not
## let it read a pipe labelled `unconfined_service_t`, which is what a service
## binary with no policy of its own gets. So the broker refuses the message,
## and every one of those commands fails.
##
## ## It does not always fail *loudly*
##
## Sometimes the broker drops the connection and `systemd-run` says "Failed to
## start transient service unit: Connection reset by peer". Sometimes the
## message is simply never relayed, no reply ever comes, and `systemd-run`
## blocks on the bus indefinitely — observed on a node as a create-pool request
## that never returned, with `systemd-run` sitting in `poll_schedule_timeout`,
## no `run-*.service` in `systemctl list-units`, and no `zpool` process at all.
##
## That second shape is why `lumen_sys::exec` bounds its wait rather than
## relying on recognising a refusal message: there is no message to recognise.
##
## ## Why this took a day to find
##
## The stock denial is `dontaudit`ed, so under `Enforcing` it produces **no
## audit record at all** — `ausearch` is empty and `getenforce` says the right
## thing. It surfaces only after `semodule -DB`. What an operator sees instead
## is `systemd-run` reporting "Failed to start transient service unit:
## Connection reset by peer", PID 1 logging nothing whatsoever (it never
## received the message — the broker killed it first), and the console saying
## "Internal server error". Three separate features — creating a pool,
## creating an account, and the restart controls — fail together and none of
## them names SELinux.
##
## ## Why the rule is this shape
##
## The daemon gains nothing here. It already has the descriptors; the grant is
## to `dbus-broker`, for reading a pipe it is being handed on purpose. Nothing
## about the unit's `ProtectSystem=strict` sandbox moves, and the privileged
## work still happens in a transient unit outside it.
##
## `read` is the denial that was actually observed. `write` and `getattr` are
## the same operation on the other two ends of the same triplet — standard
## input is a read end and standard output and error are write ends — and
## granting `read` alone would move the failure rather than fix it.
##
## The target type is broader than this daemon: `unconfined_service_t` covers
## every service without a policy of its own, not just ours. Narrowing it means
## giving the control plane a domain of its own — `lumen_controlplane_t`, with
## file contexts, a transition, and rules for PAM, `/dev/zfs`, the hypervisor
## socket, and its state directory. That is the right end state and it is a
## much larger piece of work than the bug in front of us; see docs/system.md.

module lumen-controlplane 1.0;

require {
        type system_dbusd_t;
        type unconfined_service_t;
        class fifo_file { getattr read write };
}

allow system_dbusd_t unconfined_service_t:fifo_file { getattr read write };
